3f6486f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-317722" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $\\,T\\,.$ If $\\,T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,T({v}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$, then find $T({v}_{1}).$\n\n| $T({v}_{1})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-kv+hw)=T(-kv)+T(hw)=-kT(v)+hT(w)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-6{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}) & =-6T({v}_{1})+5T({v}_{2}) \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] & =-6T({v}_{1})+5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =5[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -39\\end{aligned}] \\\\ 6T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{1}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-317722/field-1" "q-317722/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "381f7ffe-ae4b-5e45-9fa9-e2a6bd8348b3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-317722"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-74769/selection/8b8a36d96bbf76ba1018298ac259106e51224db0adb10c038acbf29fba382784" :answer/id #uuid "6d79d196-5163-5de0-bb32-5a09050f7c80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-74769/selection/b99d15be8c4865e7c043ce301a48488d6fce148875af2c104c12981465a3f8d0" :answer/id #uuid "6701a59a-df9f-57d7-8a77-cf32c4866c6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensu